Changes of this fork
====================

This file lists what the 'storage' branch of this fork adds to SnapRAID Daemon
by Andrea Mazzoleni (https://github.com/amadvance/snapraid-daemon).

The original project is licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later, and so are the
additions. Files that already existed keep the copyright of their author.
The new files (daemon/storage.c, daemon/storage.h, js/storage.js of both UIs,
test/ui/) are copyright of Robert Paleka.

The additions were written with the help of an AI coding assistant (Claude).
The AGENTS.md file of the original project asks agents to never commit to
git. That rule is followed for the original repository: nothing was committed
or proposed there. On this fork the commits were made on request of the fork
owner.


What is added
-------------

Everything is disabled by default. It is enabled by the new 'net_storage_admin'
option, and only if the API is bound to the loopback address, or it is
protected by 'net_auth_credential' or 'net_acl'. Linux only.

* Storage page in the Commander and Brutale UIs, with a diagram of the array
  and of the pool, an advisor, and a guided setup.

* Inventory of the disks, partitions, filesystems and mount points.

* Editor of the SnapRAID configuration file, with validation.

* Format (ext4, xfs) and mount of disks, with /etc/fstab kept up to date.
  A backup of the previous fstab is kept as /etc/fstab.snapraidd.bak.

* mergerfs pools: create, change and remove, with branches and policies.

* Balance of the files among the data disks, search of duplicate files in
  the branches of a pool, and a file browser.

* Recovery of failed disks, also with the array in a degraded state: fix of
  the selected disks, check, and sync, in one command.

* Arrays up to the limits of SnapRAID: six parity levels, split parity.

* Tests: 'make check' also runs a selftest of the storage code and, if
  Node.js is installed, the tests of the logic of the Storage page.


